(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-2-circulatory-physiology-2-quiz"]={"title":"Circulatory Physiology II — Physiology Exam 2","category":"Physiology","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"How do the kidneys play a dominant role in long-term arterial pressure control?","choices":["The kidneys have no relationship to long-term pressure, which is instead set entirely by baroreceptor resetting over weeks","As ECFV increases, arterial pressure increases; this rise in pressure causes the kidneys to excrete Na and water, returning ECFV to normal","Increased ECFV always decreases arterial pressure by triggering compensatory vasodilation throughout the splanchnic circulation","The kidneys only affect short-term, second-to-second pressure through rapid renin release triggered by baroreceptors"],"answer":1,"exp":"As extracellular fluid volume (ECFV) increases, arterial pressure increases; this pressure increase causes the kidneys to excrete more sodium and water (returning ECFV to normal) — a negative feedback loop that dominates long-term pressure control. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"What is the difference between pressure diuresis and pressure natriuresis?","choices":["Pressure diuresis is the effect of pressure to increase water excretion; pressure natriuresis is the effect of pressure to increase Na excretion","They are identical terms describing the kidney's response to falling blood glucose rather than arterial pressure","Pressure diuresis decreases Na excretion; pressure natriuresis decreases water excretion, both in response to falling pressure","Neither phenomenon relates to arterial pressure; both instead describe responses purely to changes in plasma osmolarity"],"answer":0,"exp":"Pressure diuresis is the effect of increased arterial pressure to increase water excretion by the kidneys, while pressure natriuresis is the effect of increased pressure to increase sodium excretion — both underlie the kidney's central role in long-term pressure regulation. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"In the graphic analysis of the renal-body fluid mechanism, what defines the equilibrium point for long-term arterial pressure?","choices":["The point where cardiac output exactly equals total peripheral resistance","The point at which aldosterone secretion reaches its maximal physiological rate","The point where the renal function curve (output) intersects the salt and water intake line","The point where mean systemic filling pressure equals right atrial pressure"],"answer":2,"exp":"The equilibrium point for long-term arterial pressure is where the renal function (output) curve intersects the salt and water intake line; the renal-body fluid feedback system is described as having an \"infinite gain\" for long-term pressure control. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Do long-term changes in total peripheral resistance (TPR) alone raise long-term arterial pressure?","choices":["Long-term pressure is entirely independent of the kidneys, being determined solely by baseline sympathetic vasoconstrictor tone alone","TPR changes only affect short-term pressure within seconds, while renal mechanisms determine every long-term pressure value alone","Yes — any sustained rise in TPR, regardless of which vascular bed happens to be affected, directly and permanently raises the long-term arterial pressure","No — changes in TPR do not affect the long-term arterial pressure level unless the renal function curve itself is altered (e.g., by changing renal vascular resistance)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Changes in total peripheral resistance (TPR) do not affect the long-term level of arterial pressure unless they alter the renal function curve — for example, changing renal vascular resistance specifically does produce long-term pressure changes, but changing resistance elsewhere in the body does not. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Where is renin synthesized and stored, and what stimulates its release?","choices":["In the zona glomerulosa of the adrenal cortex, released in response to an elevated circulating blood volume","In the posterior pituitary gland, released in response to increased plasma osmolarity","In modified smooth muscle cells of the afferent arterioles of the kidney, released in response to a fall in pressure","In hepatocytes of the liver, released in response to increased dietary sodium intake"],"answer":2,"exp":"Renin is synthesized and stored in modified smooth muscle cells in the afferent arterioles of the kidney, and is released in response to a fall in blood pressure. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Where does angiotensin I get converted to angiotensin II?","choices":["In the liver, by an enzyme secreted from hepatocyte cell membranes","In the adrenal medulla, by an enzyme co-released with epinephrine","By a converting enzyme located in endothelial cells of the pulmonary circulation","In the kidney nephron, by an enzyme secreted from the proximal tubule"],"answer":2,"exp":"Renin acts on angiotensinogen to form angiotensin I (ANG I), which is then converted to angiotensin II (ANG II) by a converting enzyme (ACE) located in the endothelial cells of the pulmonary circulation. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What are the three major actions of the renin-angiotensin system on arterial pressure regulation?","choices":["Only mild vasodilation of the coronary and cerebral circulations, with no direct renal effects","Vasodilation, increased sodium excretion, and shifting the renal function curve leftward toward lower pressures","Vasoconstriction, Na retention (via direct and indirect renal actions), and shifting the renal function curve to the right","Decreasing sympathetic activity while simultaneously suppressing aldosterone secretion from the adrenal cortex"],"answer":2,"exp":"The renin-angiotensin system causes vasoconstriction, causes sodium retention by direct and indirect actions on the kidney, and causes a rightward shift in the renal function curve — all of which help buffer drops in arterial pressure (e.g., during hemorrhage). — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which of the following factors decreases renal excretory function and increases blood pressure?","choices":["Dopamine, prostaglandins, bradykinin, and atrial natriuretic peptide","Nitric oxide, prostaglandins, dopamine, and atrial natriuretic peptide","Atrial natriuretic peptide, dopamine, nitric oxide, and bradykinin","Angiotensin II, aldosterone, sympathetic nervous activity, and endothelin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Angiotensin II, aldosterone, sympathetic nervous activity, and endothelin all decrease renal excretory function and thereby increase blood pressure, in contrast to atrial natriuretic peptide, nitric oxide, and dopamine, which increase renal excretion and reduce blood pressure. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What is the fundamental relationship between venous return and cardiac output in a steady state?","choices":["Venous return and cardiac output must be equal","They are unrelated quantities","Venous return is always greater than cardiac output","Cardiac output is always double venous return"],"answer":0,"exp":"Venous return (blood flowing from the veins into the right atrium each minute) and cardiac output (blood pumped into the aorta each minute) must be equal, since the heart cannot pump more blood than it receives, and cannot indefinitely accumulate excess blood. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What is the most important factor directly affecting cardiac output, according to this lecture?","choices":["Only physical activity, with metabolism playing no role","Age","The basic level of body metabolism","Body size alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"The basic level of body metabolism is listed as the most important direct factor affecting cardiac output, followed by physical activity, age, and body size. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What does the Frank-Starling law of the heart state?","choices":["Increased blood flow into the heart decreases contraction force by overstretching the sarcomeres beyond optimal length","Only the atria, never the ventricles, contract with increased force in response to stretch","Increased blood flow into the heart stretches the chamber walls, causing the cardiac muscle to contract with increased force","The heart's contraction force depends solely on sympathetic stimulation, independent of chamber filling volume"],"answer":2,"exp":"The Frank-Starling law of the heart states that increased quantities of blood flowing into the heart stretch the walls of the heart chambers, causing cardiac muscle to contract with increased force — the mechanistic basis of the Frank-Starling mechanism/cardiac function curve. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What is the Bainbridge reflex, and what triggers it?","choices":["A reflex triggered by stretch of the left atrium that signals the vasomotor center to gradually decrease heart rate and contractility","A reflex exclusive to the renal afferent arterioles that triggers compensatory renin release during acute hypotension","A nervous reflex triggered by stretch of the right atrium, passing to the vasomotor center and back via sympathetic/vagal nerves, that increases heart rate","A reflex that only decreases contractility via vagal fibers, with no measurable effect on sinus node firing rate at all"],"answer":2,"exp":"The Bainbridge reflex is initiated by stretch of the right atrium (e.g., from increased venous return), which sends signals to the vasomotor center in the brain, which then signals back to the heart via sympathetic and vagal nerves to increase heart rate — preventing blood from \"damming up\" in the veins/atria. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Besides the Bainbridge reflex, how does direct stretch of the sinus node affect heart rate?","choices":["Stretch of the sinus node abolishes automaticity entirely, requiring backup pacemaker activity from the AV node","It has no direct effect on rhythmicity, acting only indirectly through the Bainbridge reflex pathway","Direct stretch of the sinus node can increase heart rate by as much as 10-15%, independent of the Bainbridge reflex","It only decreases heart rate, by as much as 10-15%, through direct hyperpolarization of pacemaker cells"],"answer":2,"exp":"Stretch of the sinus node in the wall of the right atrium has a direct effect on its own rhythmicity, increasing heart rate by as much as 10-15% — a mechanism distinct from (though complementary to) the nervous Bainbridge reflex. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"How does long-term cardiac output vary with total peripheral resistance when arterial pressure is unchanged?","choices":["Cardiac output varies inversely with TPR — increased TPR decreases cardiac output, and vice versa, when other factors are held constant","TPR has no relationship to cardiac output, since output is determined solely by heart rate and stroke volume alone","Cardiac output varies directly with TPR — increased TPR raises cardiac output proportionally when pressure is held constant","Cardiac output is completely independent of TPR, being determined instead entirely by mean systemic filling pressure alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"When arterial pressure is unchanged, long-term cardiac output varies inversely with total peripheral resistance — any change in the long-term TPR level (with no other change in circulatory function) produces a cardiac output change in exactly the opposite direction. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What two mechanisms make sympathetic stimulation cause a \"hypereffective\" heart?","choices":["It decreases heart rate to as low as 40 beats per minute while simultaneously reducing contractile strength by about half","It has no effect on cardiac contractility at all, only slowing heart rate down to about 50 to 60 beats per minute at rest","It only decreases the heart rate significantly, from 72 down to as low as 20 to 40 beats per minute, leaving contractility entirely unaffected","It greatly increases heart rate (e.g., from 72 up to 180-200 beats/min) and increases the strength of contraction (contractility) to as much as twice normal"],"answer":3,"exp":"Sympathetic stimulation (with parasympathetic inhibition) creates a hypereffective heart by greatly increasing heart rate (potentially from 72 up to 180-200 beats/min in young people) and increasing contractility to as much as twice its normal strength. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What happens to cardiac output and arterial pressure during peripheral vasodilation if the sympathetic nervous system is not functioning?","choices":["Without the sympathetic nervous system, the heart cannot increase cardiac output to offset the large decrease in total peripheral resistance, so arterial pressure drops","Peripheral dilation has no effect on arterial pressure as long as renal sodium retention increases proportionally within seconds","The parasympathetic system alone fully compensates for the drop by increasing heart rate through vagal withdrawal at the sinus node","Cardiac output increases automatically through the Frank-Starling mechanism alone, and the arterial pressure returns fully to baseline within seconds"],"answer":0,"exp":"Without an intact sympathetic nervous system, the heart cannot increase cardiac output enough to offset a large decrease in total peripheral resistance from peripheral vasodilation, so arterial pressure drops significantly. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"What are the three principal factors that affect venous return to the heart?","choices":["Right atrial pressure, the degree of filling of the systemic circulation (mean systemic filling pressure), and resistance to blood flow between peripheral vessels and the right atrium","Only heart rate and stroke volume, since venous return is entirely and exclusively determined by the heart's own pumping activity alone","Only the right atrial pressure, since mean systemic filling pressure and venous resistance both remain essentially constant under all physiological conditions","Only the resistance of the pulmonary circulation, since systemic venous resistance has essentially no bearing on flow into the right atrium at all"],"answer":0,"exp":"The three principal factors affecting venous return are: right atrial pressure (a backward force impeding flow into the atrium), the degree of filling of the systemic circulation (mean systemic filling pressure, forcing blood toward the heart), and resistance to blood flow between the peripheral vessels and right atrium. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What is the mean systemic filling pressure, and what is its typical value?","choices":["The equilibrium pressure throughout the systemic circulation when all blood flow ceases, about 7 mm Hg","The peak systolic pressure recorded within the right ventricle, normally about 25 mm Hg","A theoretical pressure that only applies to the pulmonary circulation, never the systemic circulation","The pressure within the coronary sinus during diastole, normally about 5 mm Hg"],"answer":0,"exp":"Mean systemic filling pressure is the equilibrium pressure that arterial and venous pressures reach throughout the systemic circulation when all blood flow ceases, normally about 7 mm Hg; it increases as blood volume increases and as sympathetic stimulation constricts the vasculature (decreasing its capacity). — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"What proportion of the total resistance to venous return is attributable to venous resistance versus arteriolar/small artery resistance?","choices":["Venous resistance contributes essentially none, with nearly all resistance from small arteries","About two-thirds is venous resistance, about one-third is arteriolar/small artery resistance","About one-third is venous resistance, about two-thirds is capillary resistance","Both contribute equally, with each accounting for roughly half of total resistance"],"answer":1,"exp":"About two-thirds of the resistance to venous return is determined by venous resistance, and about one-third is determined by arteriolar and small artery resistance. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"In the graphical analysis of cardiac output during heavy exercise, how does the equilibrium point shift?","choices":["Right atrial pressure falls dramatically to negative values as venous return increases during exercise","Cardiac output increases substantially (e.g., to about 20 L/min) as exercise increases both the cardiac function curve and venous return curve","Exercise has no effect on the venous return curve, only shifting the cardiac function curve upward alone","Cardiac output decreases from about 5 L/min down to roughly 2 L/min as the vessels constrict throughout exercising muscle"],"answer":1,"exp":"During heavy exercise, both the cardiac function curve and the venous return curve shift, moving the equilibrium point (where venous return equals cardiac output) from a normal resting value of about 5 L/min to as high as about 20 L/min. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Using the Fick principle, if oxygen absorption by the lungs is 200 mL/min and the arteriovenous oxygen difference is 40 mL/L, what is the calculated cardiac output?","choices":["8000 L/min","40 L/min","2 L/min","5 L/min"],"answer":3,"exp":"By the Fick principle, cardiac output = oxygen absorbed by the lungs / arteriovenous oxygen difference = 200 mL/min ÷ 40 mL/L = 5 L/min, matching the normal resting cardiac output. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"By approximately how many times can skeletal muscle blood flow increase from rest to extreme exercise?","choices":["12-25x","No increase occurs","100x","2-3x"],"answer":0,"exp":"Skeletal muscle blood flow can increase from about 3-4 mL/min/100g at rest to 50-80 mL/min/100g during extreme exercise — a 12-25 fold increase, driven by local metabolite-triggered vasodilation and opening of previously dormant capillaries. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"What is functional hyperemia?","choices":["A decrease in blood flow triggered by sympathetic vasoconstriction during rest","An increase in blood flow seen only within resting cardiac muscle tissue","A pathological increase in blood flow occurring only during sleep","The increase in blood flow that occurs when a muscle becomes active"],"answer":3,"exp":"Functional hyperemia is the increase in blood flow that occurs when a muscle becomes metabolically active, driven by local metabolites (lactic acid, ATP, K+, CO2) triggering vasodilation in response to reduced local oxygen availability. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"What is the role of sympathetic vasoconstrictor nerve activation during exercise, given that it can attenuate local vasodilation?","choices":["It has no functional role during exercise, since sympathetic outflow to skeletal muscle vasculature is completely suppressed once exercise starts","It acts as a \"brake\" preventing excess dilation in exercising muscle (triggered by metabolic factors), while also constricting other vascular beds and non-exercising muscle to redistribute blood flow","It only affects the coronary and cerebral vasculature, having essentially no effect whatsoever upon skeletal muscle or the splanchnic vessels during exertion","It completely overrides all local metabolic vasodilation, fully shutting down blood flow to exercising muscle no matter how high oxygen demand rises"],"answer":1,"exp":"Sympathetic vasoconstrictor nerve activation can attenuate (but not eliminate) the local metabolic vasodilation in exercising muscle, acting as a \"brake\" against excess dilation, while simultaneously constricting other vascular beds and non-exercising muscle to redistribute blood flow to where it's needed most. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"What is the main stimulus for the increased cardiac output seen during exercise?","choices":["Increased sympathetic activity, along with increased venous return and increased arterial pressure","Parasympathetic stimulation alone, along with decreased venous return and lowered arterial pressure","Decreased sympathetic activity, along with reduced venous return and reduced myocardial contractility","A decrease in venous return caused by pooling of blood within the dilated splanchnic circulation"],"answer":0,"exp":"During exercise, cardiac output increases mainly due to increased sympathetic activity, along with increased venous return (from venoconstriction, increased mean systemic filling pressure, and decreased resistance in exercising muscle) and increased arterial pressure — since a healthy heart pumps out what it receives. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What is the cardiac index, and what is its approximate normal value for a typical 70 kg adult?","choices":["Total blood volume divided by body surface area, approximately 3 L/m2","Cardiac output divided by heart rate, approximately 100 mL/beat at rest","Cardiac output per square meter of body surface area, approximately 3 L/min/m2","Stroke volume adjusted for body weight, approximately 1 mL/kg per beat"],"answer":2,"exp":"The cardiac index is cardiac output stated per square meter of body surface area; for a 70 kg human with a body surface area of about 1.7 m², the cardiac index is approximately 3 L/min/m2. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"How does cardiac index change from childhood to old age, and what does this reflect?","choices":["It rises rapidly to over 4 L/min/m2 around age 10 and declines to about 2.4 L/min/m2 by age 80, reflecting declining metabolic activity/muscle mass with age","Cardiac index has no relationship to metabolic activity, being determined instead solely by adult body surface area","Cardiac index remains completely constant at about 3 L/min/m2 from infancy right through old age, entirely unaffected by growth or ageing","Cardiac index steadily increases from about 2 L/min/m2 at birth to over 5 L/min/m2 by age 80, tracking rising body mass"],"answer":0,"exp":"Cardiac index rises rapidly to a level greater than 4 L/min/m2 around age 10 and declines to about 2.4 L/min/m2 by age 80, since cardiac output is regulated throughout life in near-direct proportion to overall metabolic activity — the decline reflects declining activity/muscle mass with age. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which of the following would cause a \"hypoeffective\" heart (decreased pumping ability)?","choices":["Decreased arterial pressure combined with increased vagal excitation of the sinoatrial node","Increased sympathetic activity always produces a hypoeffective heart, regardless of the coronary status","Increased arterial pressure (severe hypertension), coronary artery blockage, valvular heart disease, and cardiac hypoxia","Increased venous return alone, without any change in coronary perfusion or valve function"],"answer":2,"exp":"Factors causing a hypoeffective heart include increased arterial pressure (e.g., severe hypertension) against which the heart must pump, inhibition of nervous excitation, abnormal heart rhythm, coronary artery blockage (heart attack), valvular or congenital heart disease, myocarditis, and cardiac hypoxia. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"How does strong sympathetic stimulation affect mean circulatory filling pressure?","choices":["It only affects the filling pressure within the pulmonary circulation, leaving the systemic vessels and their capacity unchanged","It decreases mean circulatory filling pressure by dilating veins and thereby increasing overall vascular capacity","It increases mean circulatory filling pressure by constricting systemic blood vessels, decreasing the system's capacity at each blood volume level","It has no effect on mean circulatory filling pressure, since sympathetic tone only alters arteriolar resistance"],"answer":2,"exp":"Strong sympathetic stimulation constricts systemic blood vessels, decreasing the capacity of the vascular system so that, at each level of blood volume, the mean circulatory filling pressure is increased. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"How does an increase in mean systemic filling pressure (Psf) affect the venous return curve?","choices":["It shifts the venous return curve upward and to the right","It shifts the curve's plateau downward without changing its slope","It steepens the slope of the curve without shifting its plateau","It only affects the curve's slope, never its plateau value"],"answer":0,"exp":"The greater the mean systemic filling pressure (Psf) — reflecting how \"tightly\" the circulatory system is filled with blood — the more the venous return curve shifts upward and to the right; conversely, a lower Psf shifts the curve downward and to the left. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 32"}]};
